const pageData = JSON.parse("{\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"title\":\"Measurement\",\"eyebrow\":\"\",\"introduction\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"contentHtml\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eMeasurement. Almost all physics textbook that I'm exposed to starts with this concept as the first unit, convincing me that we shall set this as the starting point of our general knowledge section.\\n(I know this section resembles normal textbook, yet I promise that it won't be boring as you originally thought. I promise. And these sections will be updated over time. As you can see, currently, my first step is applying an informal tone so that everyone feels no pressure at all. Anyway, let's begin.)\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSo, you may find that this section is specifically tied to Newtonian Mechanics. This is because that traditional measurement is totally different from modern quantum-level measurement, thus explicitly stating this distinction at the start so that no one's confused. And since we've mentioned that there's a huge distinction between traditional and modern measurement, what's the distinction? The distinction is that the result of traditional measurement is always finite and static: two associated physical quantities can be obtained simultaneously; but at quantum level, this simultaneity is not promised. The most famous principle is Heisenberg's uncertainty principle: one cannot obtain precise measurement result of poisition and momentum simultaneously (or energy and time): if one value is made precise, the other one will be ridiculously wrong (the uncertainty increases dramatically).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eBack to Newtonian measurement, we haven't explicitly and rigorously define measurement yet. Think about life examples of measurement: every time, when you're sick, you would place a thermometer containing mercury into your mouth or under your armpit to get the body temperature; you will utilize a meterstick or measuring tape to get your height; your PE teacher relies on a stop watch to record the time spent for each student to complete 800/1,000 m run. All these are familiar scenarios in which you're measuring things.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThe most essential basis of measurement is establishing a standard, or namely a metric. On a meterstick, we can find multiple evenly-spaced interval, and it's the value assigned to each of these lines yielding the final result of measurement. Two metersticks can adopt distinctive measurement metrics (i.e. the difference between inch and meter), thus giving us two different values. Yet we know that the length of a measured object never changes by simply switching from one ruler to another, which is another essential concept: the measurement is a relative and arbitrary process, yet the measured quantity has an intrinsic consistency regardless of change of measurement tools. Accordingly, one metric can be converted into another metric, as long as they're used for describing the same physical quantity (you never see that someone converts celsius degree into milimeter).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eBesides, another important concept of  measurement is that, since measurement is relative and arbitrary, the result is only an approximation (so that no matter how advanced your chosen tool is, the final result has certain error from the real value). Suppose that you're measuring the length of your shoes using a meterstick, the scale of the meterstick is 1cm. Then, the obtained value shall be with last digit as one decimal place after 1cm (i.e. 28.1cm). This last digit is an approximation, it's assumption instead of reality. The real length of the shoe may be some value as 28.17532cm, but, due to the limitation of your measurement tools, you cannot have a result with such precision. Also, I need to clarify that more advanced measurement instrument can only help you improve precision and approximating the real value closer, yet it can never send you the real value. Thus, measurement is a process of approximation based on relative unit applied.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eNext, we're introducing two seemingly identical yet very different concepts: precision and accuracy. Using a very classical model--a target and several darts--accuracy is that the darts hit the target with a closer distance from the center of the target, while precision is that the clump of darts has a smaller radius so that they're more locally concentrated. Therefore, measurement result can be accurate but not precise (the previous example of measuring your own shoe); the result can be precise but not accurate (even if you have the best ruler in the world and if you use it with a tilted angle from the measured object, the result may largely deviate from the expected value); the result can be neither precise nor accurate (basically combining the previous two situations); it can be both precise and accurate (which is our aimed goal in study of physics). Later, we'll encounter tons of opportunities demanding your own design of experiments in which measurement takes up a large portion. And we've probably figured out that problem of accuracy is easier to be avoided by refining specific measurement processes, but the problem of precision depends on the device used.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd similarly, errors can be sorted into different types: random error, systematic error, and gross error. Indeed, these are less important concepts--I put them here simply for ones who're interested in it. Random error, suggested by its name, is the type of error that is unpredictable (suppose that you're measuring the room temperature, the result may vary randomly: 25.27 celsius degree, 25.33 celsius degree...); systematic error is the type of error that occurs in the design of measurement process (suppose that you mistakenly repeat the process of measurement or complicate the process with increasing error); finally, gross error is the type of error with another nick name, human error (it's not necessarily always human error, though. It happens when your hands are shaking or when you're at a tilted angle from the horizontal level line of a liquid container). Hence, the later two errors can be eliminated by improving the measurement design, but the first type is harder to avoid.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThat's all, the most important elements of Newtonian measurement. It's like an instrutional manual, I know, since this is the very beginning of physics (like learning a computer language at the first time, some boring but necessary things need to be remembered). Indeed, this would help form better habits.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Basis of Measurement \\u0026 Units\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/basis-of-measurement-and-units/\"}");
